it's really easy to set up auto scaling  on your google cloud managed instance  groups the configuration options offer  lots of flexibility to help fit your  application's needs let's dive a little  deeper into auto scaling and check out  three things that will help you set up  effective auto scaling on your instance  groups  [Music]  to implement more effective auto scaling  it's helpful to understand how it  generally works  the auto scaler monitors your instance  group and offers a size recommendation  that's a calculated number of vms needed  to serve the peak load over the last 10  minutes  these 10 minutes are referred to as the  stabilization period  this stabilization period is 10 minutes  so the auto scaler can ensure that usage  info that it uses to scale in and out is  stable and that helps prevent the auto  scaler from adding and removing machines  at an excessive break  when scaling back down it's able to  determine the smaller group size will be  able to support the peak load so that it  can safely remove instances  when auto scaling is turned on the  number of vms it scales your instance  group up and down to will be limited by  the minimum and maximum number of vms  that you specify when scaling in the  number of vms it can remove is also  limited by the scale in controls if  specified  if you configure auto scaling and leave  it turned off it still calculates  recommendations so you can see what the  auto scaler would do if it's turned on  without having to spin up additional  machines in the process  this way you can see the effects it'll  have before actually committing to them  so now that you know a little bit about  how it works here are three things to  consider when designing your auto  scaling configuration thing number one  the machine type okay so technically  this isn't part of the auto scale  configuration you actually indicate the  machine type on the instance template  associated with your instance group  but that's why it's so important for  auto scaling a new instance of that type  will be provisioned when the instance  group is scaled up  you might be thinking well yeah of  course but this is when knowing your  application is key for example if you  know that your application that you're  running on your instance group needs two  virtual cpus then e2 standard 2 has got  you covered  but if you also know that the same  application doesn't need much memory  then e2 high cpu 2 offers the same  number of virtual cpus with less memory  choosing that one means that every time  you scale your instance group out you're  not scaling out or paying for resources  that you won't be using  okay thing two  the cooldown period for effective auto  scaling you want to make sure you  specify an accurate cooldown period  the cooldown period is the  initialization time the amount of time  it takes for your application to  initialize after the host vm has been  provisioned  you want to be sure that you have an  accurate one specified  if you specify a cooldown period that's  too long then your auto scaler will miss  out on that utilization data and  potentially underestimate the required  size of your group  on the flip side if you specify a  cooldown period that's shorter than it  takes for your application to be ready  then it's possible for the auto scaler  to get inaccurate utilization data  because in some cases cpu utilization  while things are getting up and running  is higher and isn't a reflection of  normal use finally there's thing three  scaling controls  the ability for your managed instance  group to scale is a big win for reducing  costs  but you don't want to take a hit in  performance when you do that  scaling controls let you slow things  down by establishing a rate that the  auto skiller will scale down at this way  you'll be able to avoid dramatic drops  in group size remember that cooldown  period i just mentioned before  if your cooldown period is on the long  side by using scaling controls you'll  have the added benefit when there's a  sudden spike in demand you'll already  have machines available and you can  avoid delays caused by long  initializations  setting up the right scaling controls  just like choosing the right machine and  accurate cooldown period all comes down  to knowing your application its  requirements and expected load and  behavior  let's take a look at this managed  instance group that i've set up on  google cloud project here's a few things  about my configuration  i've set my minimum instances to 2 and  my maximum to 100 when i set this up i  wasn't sure about cooldown period so i  left it at the default value of 60  seconds  i wasn't sure about scaling controls so  i left that disabled  the instance template uses an e2  standard 2.  let's see how we can make changes to  this configuration let's edit my manage  instance group called web app instance  group and check out the auto scale  settings  we've got a regional managed instance  group so right off the bat i want to  take advantage of high availability by  making sure that i've got at least one  instance in each of my zones  that's three zones so i'll increase my  minimum to three  now that i'm here i also want to make  sure that my maximum is set to a  reasonable number  although it may be unlikely for my  application to need to scale that high  it's still possible for it to go as high  as 100.  for my application this is too much so  let's lower that to 30. it takes the  application of my virtual machine two  minutes to be ready so i'll set the  cooldown period to 120 seconds  that's pretty long so i'm going to  enable scaling controls in case i get  any spikes when my group is scaling down  in this case my instance group won't  scale down by more than 10 vms from the  peak size as observed over the prior 10  minutes  i'm going to save these settings for now  before i change one more thing  the vm type specified in the template is  an e2 standard 2 but that's got more  memory than i need so let's see how i  can replace that with an e2 high cpu2  instead  rather than clicking edit on my instance  group i can instead go up here to update  vms  up top here it says the current size of  my instance group  and the name of the template i use to  create the instances in it  here under new template i could add a  second template and roll that out to  only a certain percentage of my instance  group  this is one way to do a b testing or  deploy a canary of my application in  this case i want to replace my only  template with a different one that i  created for this demonstration with one  template specified my target size is 100  of my instance group down here under  update configuration i can specify how  i'd like the template to be rolled out  by selecting automatic the manage  instance group will proactively tear  down and replace each of my instance  group's instances created with the old  template here where it says temporary  additional instances i can specify how  many machines beyond the recommended  number can be provisioned to make this  transition possible this makes it  possible to maintain the recommended  number of vms while still tearing down  and recreating them  down here at the bottom is an update  summary that recaps configuration i set  for this update  once i'm finished i can kick this update  off using the equivalent command line or  rest api calls  otherwise i can just click update vms  here  the auto scaling offer to manage  instance groups is very flexible  the cooldown period and scaling controls  are a great start to setting up  effective auto scaling  also don't forget to choose a properly  sized vm on your instance template  beyond that there's much more you can  learn from the documentation to make  sure that your auto scaling is working  for you efficiently effectively and  affordably to learn more check out this  link for more on auto scaling and check  out the link in the description for  another demo on auto scaling  thanks for watching  [Music]  you
 

